    "ACPWB's {area} process includes a verification step that requires comparison of transaction data against {count} reference documents, each maintained in a different system with a different version control model. Handlers performing this verification must navigate between {count} systems and reconcile potentially conflicting reference data before proceeding — a cognitive burden that contributes to processing errors and disproportionately impacts newer team members.",
    "A review of {area} operating procedures found that {pct}% of procedures contain undefined terms — role names, system references, and technical concepts — not explained within the document or linked to a reference glossary. Handlers who encounter these terms must seek clarification through informal channels, introducing interpretation variation and processing delays.",
    "The {area} process includes {count} manual quality checks performed by the same individuals who execute the processing steps being checked. This segregation-of-duties gap has been noted in {years} consecutive internal audit reports and creates a control environment where quality problems are systematically underreported because they are identified — when at all — by the individuals responsible for them.",
    "ACPWB's {area} process has not been assessed for automation readiness since {old_year}, despite significant advances in the technology platforms available to the organization and documented improvements in rule-based decision logic that would qualify additional transaction types for automated routing. An up-to-date automation readiness assessment would identify {count} transaction types currently handled manually that meet established criteria for automated processing.",
    "The {area} process currently requires {count} sequential review and approval steps for transactions above a defined threshold, yet the sequence was designed without considering the information availability and decision context of each reviewer. Several approval steps are performed by individuals who do not have access to all the information required to make an informed decision.",
    "A capacity analysis of the {area} function found that aggregate team capacity is sufficient to process average daily transaction volumes with {pct}% capacity headroom, yet backlog accumulation is a persistent and documented problem. The apparent paradox is explained by the distribution of capacity across the team — {count} high-volume handlers are consistently overloaded while an equivalent number operate below optimal utilization.",
